{"score": 45, "summary": "The module has several concrete security risks. The Flask fine-tuning server runs in debug mode by default, which exposes an interactive debugger. API keys are retrieved from inconsistent config paths across files, and the fine-tuning endpoint has no authentication guard. Sensitive API request failure bodies (potentially containing key details) are logged verbatim.", "findings": [{"id": "SEC-001", "severity": "critical", "category": "security", "title": "Flask fine-tuning server runs with debug=True in production", "description": "bin/fine_tuning.py (and its duplicate scripts/fine_tuning.py) starts Flask with `app.run(debug=True)`. This enables Werkzeug's interactive debugger, which allows arbitrary Python code execution via the browser if an exception is raised. Anyone who can reach the service can execute OS-level commands.", "file": "bin/fine_tuning.py", "line": 121, "snippet": "if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)", "fix": "Remove the debug flag or set it from an environment variable: `app.run(debug=os.environ.get('FLASK_DEBUG', 'false').lower() == 'true')`. Never deploy with debug=True."}，{"id": "SEC-002", "severity": "high", "category": "security", "title": "Fine-tuning Flask endpoint has no authentication", "description": "The `/fine-tune` POST route in bin/fine_tuning.py accepts arbitrary model fine-tuning requests with no API key, token, or authentication middleware. Any network-reachable actor can trigger expensive GPU training jobs or exfiltrate the fine-tuned model.", "file": "bin/fine_tuning.py", "line": 92, "snippet": "@app.route('/fine-tune', methods=['POST'])
def fine_tune():", "fix": "Add a shared-secret header check or use Flask-Login/an API key middleware before processing the request."}, {"id": "SEC-003", "severity": "high", "category": "security", "title": "OpenAI API key read from three inconsistent config paths", "description": "The API key is read from `services.openai.api_key` (AIService, GeneratePredictionsAction), `openai.api_key` (GeneratePredictionDraftsAction), and `ai.openai_api_key` (MakeAIRequestAction). If the wrong path resolves to empty string, requests fail silently or fall back; if the wrong path accidentally leaks a value, a different key is used than intended.", "file": "app/Actions/Support/MakeAIRequestAction.php", "line": 24, "snippet": "$apiKey = config('ai.openai_api_key', '');", "fix": "Standardise on a single config key (e.g. `services.openai.api_key`) and reference it in one place, injected via the service container."}, {"id": "SEC-004", "severity": "medium", "category": "security", "title": "Full OpenAI error response body logged verbatim", "description": "When an OpenAI API call fails, the raw response body is written to the log (GeneratePredictionsAction:152-154 and AIChatCompletionClient:76-79). Error bodies from OpenAI can contain rate-limit context, org IDs, or reflected request fragments. Logging them verbatim risks leaking sensitive metadata.", "file": "app/Actions/GeneratePredictionsAction.php", "line": 154, "snippet": "Log::error('OpenAI API request failed', [
    'status' => $response->status(),
    'body' => $response->body(),
]);", "fix": "Log only the HTTP status code and a fixed error message. Omit or truncate the raw body: `'body' => substr($response->body(), 0, 200)`."}]}

